{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed807260302/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6bcdb01e\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nExtend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.\n- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.\n- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.\n- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.\n\nKey insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.\n- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.\n- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b1986d4b\" lines=\"19-37\">\n<description>\nRender the filled-square object counts as compact nested stacks ordered by count rank.\n\nInput structure\nA large noisy grid has a dominant background, scattered non-square noise, and\nfilled monochrome square objects. Each square side length has exactly one\nobject color, and side lengths form consecutive sizes.\n\nRule\nIgnore non-square components. If the lowest square in the scene is of the\nsmallest side length, treat that one smallest square as a distractor and do\nnot count it. For count rank k from left to right, create one block from all\nsquare sizes with at least k remaining objects; draw larger squares first and\nsmaller squares over their upper-left corner, then append one background\nseparator column.\n\nKey insight\nThe output is a count histogram of square side lengths, but each histogram\nslot is rendered as nested upper-left square masks using the side colors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3ad05f52\" lines=\"128-142\">\n<description>\nPaint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.\n- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.\n\nRule\n- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.\n- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.\n- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.\n\nKey insight\n- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"79cce52d\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nCrop off the control row and column, then cyclically roll the payload by the two marker offsets.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is square, with a control row on top and a control column on the left.\n- The control row and column share one filler color and one single-pixel marker color.\n- The lower-right square is the payload and contains none of the control colors.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top control row and left control column.\n- Roll the remaining payload down by the marker's zero-based position in the left control column.\n- Roll the payload right by the marker's zero-based position in the top control row.\n\nKey insight\n- The two border markers are cyclic shift amounts for the payload, not colors to preserve.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8a6d367c\" lines=\"52-67\">\n<description>\nComplete the framed area by expanding the unique upper template that contains the existing seed pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- A teal background contains small colored template masks above a larger rectangular frame.\n- The frame encloses one fill color arranged as a partial block-scaled copy of one template.\n- The frame interior dimensions are an integer square scale of the matching template bbox.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the rectangular frame.\n- Identify the unique upper template whose block-scaled mask covers every existing fill pixel inside the frame.\n- Reset the frame interior to background and paint the whole scaled mask with the fill color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe lower colored pixels are seed cells that reveal which upper template should be completed inside the frame.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52364a65\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\n\nRule:\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7ed72f31\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nMirror colored sprites using red (2) markers as reflection axes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) = marker color.\n- Colored sprites appear near red markers.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
